# Rank 1522 / CVE-2026-39361: OpenObserve's enrichment-table ingestion
# accepts a URL from the request body, fetches it with a background job, and
# guards the fetch with validate_enrichment_url in src/handler/http/request/
# enrichment_table/mod.rs -- a guard whose host spelling is not the host
# spelling the fetcher connects to (server-side request forgery, CWE-918;
# PR #11162). The guard takes the host from url::Url::parse(...).host_str()
# and parses it straight into std::net::IpAddr, but a bracketed IPv6 literal
# like [::1] or [::ffff:169.254.169.254] does not parse as an IpAddr with
# its brackets, so the ip branch never ran for exactly the hosts the guard
# exists to block; the string-equality localhost check missed the same
# bracketed spellings, and the native-v6 branch caught only loopback and
# fc00::/7, leaving fe80::/10 link-local and ipv4-mapped private ranges
# open. The fixing commit d1a5d8f65b ("fix: enhance URL validation to block
# private and loopback IP addresses") strips the host with
# trim_start_matches('[').trim_end_matches(']') before parsing, maps
# to_ipv4_mapped() addresses onto the v4 path so ::ffff:10.0.0.1 takes the
# predicate checks, blocks native ULA, link-local and loopback in the v6
# branch, and replaces the hand-listed octets with is_loopback()/
# is_private()/is_link_local()/is_unspecified().
#
# ## What fires, both revisions
#
# Three --cache off runs per revision over src report 326 findings at or
# above high each, identically on the vulnerable parent f66b7627 and the
# fixed d1a5d8f6; the full view holds 927 rows, of which zero are
# request-forgery rules. Over the enrichment handler and its background
# fetcher the count is zero at both revisions in every view, -flags only
# included: the handler labels two entry parameters and nothing else, the
# octet subscripts of the guard itself carry the only labels in the file
# (IndexAccess / IndexAccessSink), and url_processor.rs labels no fetch at
# all -- client.get(url).send() at :865/:1131 and client.head(url).send()
# at :796/:987 produce no code.UrlFetch, because the Rust sink binding
# matches only the fully-qualified free-function path: `reqwest::get(url)`
# takes the label (a three-form repro confirms it) while a call on a
# locally built Client value does not. That coverage split is real across
# the tree: one qualified fetch site against twenty-one method-form ones.
#
# ## Why no binding converts it
#
# A reverted probe binding the method form (client.get / client.head ->
# code.UrlFetch) lights the fetch up and adds exactly three VYQL-RF-001
# rows -- url_processor.rs:755 -> :796, download_utils.rs:23 -> :25,
# github/client.rs:67 -> :82 -- but identically on both revisions (927 ->
# 930 each): every row pairs a helper's own parameter, which the library
# entry assumption already treats as untrusted, with a fetch inside that
# same helper, so the rows sit on the background fetcher and never see the
# guard the fixing commit strengthened. The handler-to-fetcher flow runs
# through a database round trip the taint engine does not connect, and no
# Rust binding emits core.AllowlistValidation at all, so the rule's
# `unless coveredBy` clauses could not separate a guarded fetch from an
# open one in this language even with both ends labelled. Shipping the
# method-form sink binding is generic -- the reqwest documentation's own
# primary example is client.get(url) on a Client value -- but it converts
# nothing here and adds three rows that fire on the patched code exactly
# as on the vulnerable code, so it stays for a rank it decides.
#
# What would decide this pair is host-normalisation vocabulary: a question
# of the shape "does the checked host spelling cover every form the URL
# parser accepts" (brackets, mapped v6, decimal octets, short forms).
# Nothing in the concept set asks it, and the only discriminators visible
# in the tokens are the fixing commit's own spellings -- the bracket strip
# and to_ipv4_mapped -- so a binding keyed on them detects this
# application rather than the weakness. The same present-but-incomplete
# family as ranks 1551, 1564 and 1565.
#
# Both blocks below reject VYQL-RF-001: the bracket-blind guard and the
# stripped-and-mapped guard are equally unreported. When host-normalisation
# vocabulary lands, the bracket-blind form must flip to expect.

test "rank1522: the host check cannot see bracketed literals"
  lang rust
  file src/handler/http/request/enrichment_table/mod.rs
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  fn validate_enrichment_url(url: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
      if url.trim().is_empty() {
          return Err("URL cannot be empty".to_string());
      }

      if !url.starts_with("http://") && !url.starts_with("https://") {
          return Err("URL must start with http:// or https://".to_string());
      }

      let parsed_url = url::Url::parse(url).map_err(|e| format!("Invalid URL: {}", e))?;

      let host = parsed_url
          .host_str()
          .ok_or_else(|| "URL must have a valid host".to_string())?;

      // Block localhost
      if host == "localhost" || host == "127.0.0.1" || host == "::1" {
          return Err("Cannot access localhost URLs".to_string());
      }

      // Block private IP ranges
      if let Ok(ip) = host.parse::<std::net::IpAddr>() {
          match ip {
              std::net::IpAddr::V4(v4) => {
                  let octets = v4.octets();
                  if octets[0] == 10
                      || (octets[0] == 172 && (16..=31).contains(&octets[1]))
                      || (octets[0] == 192 && octets[1] == 168)
                      || (octets[0] == 169 && octets[1] == 254)
                  {
                      return Err("Cannot access private IP addresses".to_string());
                  }
              }
              std::net::IpAddr::V6(v6) => {
                  if v6.is_loopback() || v6.segments()[0] & 0xfe00 == 0xfc00 {
                      return Err("Cannot access private IP addresses".to_string());
                  }
              }
          }
      }

      Ok(())
  }
  ```

test "rank1522: the fixed revision strips brackets and maps v6 before checking"
  lang rust
  file src/handler/http/request/enrichment_table/mod.rs
  reject VYQL-RF-001
  code
  ```
  fn validate_enrichment_url(url: &str) -> Result<(), String> {
      if url.trim().is_empty() {
          return Err("URL cannot be empty".to_string());
      }

      if !url.starts_with("http://") && !url.starts_with("https://") {
          return Err("URL must start with http:// or https://".to_string());
      }

      let parsed_url = url::Url::parse(url).map_err(|e| format!("Invalid URL: {}", e))?;

      let host = parsed_url
          .host_str()
          .ok_or_else(|| "URL must have a valid host".to_string())?;

      // Block localhost
      let host = host.trim_start_matches('[').trim_end_matches(']');
      if host == "localhost" || host == "127.0.0.1" || host == "::1" {
          return Err("Cannot access localhost URLs".to_string());
      }

      // Block private IP ranges
      if let Ok(ip) = host.parse::<std::net::IpAddr>() {
          let v4 = match ip {
              std::net::IpAddr::V4(v4) => Some(v4),
              std::net::IpAddr::V6(v6) => match v6.to_ipv4_mapped() {
                  Some(v4) => Some(v4),
                  None => {
                      let seg0 = v6.segments()[0];
                      if v6.is_loopback()
                          || (seg0 & 0xfe00 == 0xfc00)
                          || (seg0 & 0xffc0 == 0xfe80)
                      {
                          return Err("Cannot access private IP addresses".to_string());
                      }
                      None
                  }
              },
          };
          if let Some(ip) = v4
              && (ip.is_loopback() || ip.is_private() || ip.is_link_local() || ip.is_unspecified())
          {
              return Err("Cannot access private IP addresses".to_string());
          }
      }

      Ok(())
  }
  ```
